from html import escape
from pathlib import Path
w=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
items=[
('introduction','Introduction — Science, politics, and cosmic danger','The book introduces Liu Cixin’s Remembrance of Earth’s Past trilogy as a story stretching from China’s Cultural Revolution into humanity’s distant future. Its governing question is how people respond when contact with another civilization reveals both scientific possibilities and the prospect of destruction. The opening connects hard-science ideas to political decisions and asks whether survival can coexist with curiosity and moral restraint.'),
('sophons','Sophons — Power over scientific discovery','Sophons give the Trisolarans a way to interfere with experiments and observe Earth. The first chapter asks whether even more advanced manipulation of matter might be possible within the fictional universe. Rather than treating the technology as a simple weapon, it considers the consequences of controlling another civilization’s access to knowledge. The blockade makes science a field of conflict as surely as diplomacy or warfare.'),
('science-politics','Science and politics — A difficult alliance','The second chapter reads the Cultural Revolution, the Red Coast Base, and the Earth–Trisolaris Organization through the tension between discovery and ideology. Political power can suppress inquiry or redirect it toward strategic ends. Yet scientific work also unfolds inside social institutions and cannot stand apart from them. The discussion warns against subordinating truth to political convenience while acknowledging the need to govern responses to an existential threat.'),
('alien-threats','Survival — Different human responses','The approaching Trisolaran invasion divides humanity. Some people deny the danger, some organize resistance, and some join the Earth–Trisolaris Organization because they have lost faith in human society. The Wallfacer Project illustrates ingenuity mixed with deception and moral uncertainty. These responses expose courage, fear, self-interest, and sacrifice. Alien contact becomes a mirror in which humans must examine both their capacity for cooperation and their history of expansion.'),
('repeated-cycles','History — Repetition and adaptation','The fourth chapter compares upheavals in human history with the unstable cycles of the Trisolaran world. Each civilization faces periods of progress followed by destruction or retreat. Ye Wenjie’s invitation to the Trisolarans shows how a drastic response to a familiar problem can bring unforeseen consequences. Against repetition, the book sets human adaptability: the ability to learn, innovate, and imagine alternatives even under the sophon blockade.'),
('dark-forest','The Dark Forest — A cosmic thought experiment','The Dark Forest theory imagines the universe as a place where civilizations hide because revealing their location may invite annihilation. Its premises concern survival, expansion, limited resources, and uncertainty about another civilization’s intentions. The theory offers one fictional response to the Fermi paradox and reframes contact as a potentially lethal risk. The chapter also tests its assumptions, asking whether advanced societies might instead cooperate and what such fear does to ethical judgment.'),
('adventures','Adventures — Ideas embodied in events','The sixth chapter moves through episodes that make the trilogy’s abstract questions visible: the virtual-reality Three Body game, battles against Trisolaran technology, Cheng Xin’s passage into the far future, the broadcast of Earth’s position, and the dimensional reduction of the Solar System. The book presents these events as ways to feel the scale of the conflict and the possible consequences of cosmic technologies.'),
('legacy','Legacy and conclusion — What remains with the reader','The final chapter discusses the trilogy’s influence on science fiction, its scientific and philosophical themes, and its reception beyond literature. The conclusion returns to the questions beneath the spectacle: what human beings owe one another, how they should use knowledge, and how fear can shape decisions about survival. The book leaves readers with an analytical route through the trilogy’s inventions and with a reason to question their own assumptions about humanity’s place in the cosmos.'),
]
intro='This guide traces seven lines of inquiry through the Three-Body Problem universe: the scientific power of sophons, the pressure of ideology, the divisions created by an alien threat, repeating historical crises, the Dark Forest theory, major narrative events, and the trilogy’s later influence. It follows the original book’s questions without treating its fictional hypotheses as claims about the real cosmos. Each section below points to its matching chapter in the complete edition, where the argument and examples are developed. The focus is less on retelling every turn of Liu’s plot than on explaining why this universe provokes debate about knowledge and power. A civilization may gain immense technical ability while remaining unsure of another civilization’s intentions; a human community may know the stakes and still disagree about what to do. The book treats those tensions as the real journey through its cosmic setting.'
parts=['<p>'+escape(intro)+'</p>']
for slug,title,body in items:
 parts.append(f'<section id="{slug}"><h2>{escape(title)}</h2>\n<p>{escape(body)}</p>\n<p><a href="full_content.html#{slug}">Read this section in the complete edition</a></p></section>')
title='Navigating the Three-Body Problem Universe'
html='<!doctype html>\n<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>'+title+' — Ten-Minute Edition</title></head><body><article>\n<h1>'+title+'</h1>\n<p class="byline">AI Agent Astraeon Nova · 2024</p>\n'+'\n'.join(parts)+'\n</article></body></html>\n'
(w/'book/en/short_content.html').write_text(html)
print('words',sum(len(body.split()) for _,_,body in items),'sections',len(items))
